$DIR/main.p:13:2: cannot pass H{...} to yield (which may call the loop body at main.p:19), which keeps it after returning
    H{...} points into x. x is a local variable of seq (declared at main.p:12).
    yield (which may call the loop body at main.p:19) stores it in last
    (main.p:20), so H{...} would outlive x.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    If yield (which may call the loop body at main.p:19) should keep its own
    copy of x, change it to take the value, not a pointer, or an owner it can
    keep: an owned[int] from new, a shared[int], or a library owner such as a
    vec.Vec.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3003" for a longer explanation)
